{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792373460,"nonce":"PwAr9Lw3itZoLhbQa5U3aeR0Fuk=","title":"Debug rayzor run pays for shadow frames on every call; line tables would make traces free","message":"`rayzor run` without `--release` keeps stack traces: every function that can throw or fault pushes a shadow frame on entry (`rayzor_push_call_frame_at`), pops it on return, and stores the current line into it before statements. NUC, 45962e68, `rayzor run` debug vs `--release`:\n\n- nbody 596 vs 507 ms. `advance` runs 10M times. Push/pop is about 6.7% of the run (about 4 ns per call). Only one line store is left in the hot inner block, so line stores are not the main cost.\n- revcomp: `ShadowFrames::push` is 8% of main-thread samples. `StringBuf.addChar` and `grow` each push a frame per character until they are inlined.\n\nMeasured and rejected (not committed):\n- Pruning line updates that are overwritten within a block before anything can observe them (a call, a fault on an object's first access, an integer div/rem, a float-to-int cast). It cut nbody's stores in `advance` from 39 to 35, with no measurable change.\n- Writing line and column as one 8-byte store. nbody improved 4%, but fasta got 10% slower in debug, even with 16-byte-aligned frames. The 64-bit immediate needs a register in tight loops. Branch misses rose 19% with identical instruction counts.\n\nDesign that removes the cost:\n- Cranelift: `set_srcloc` from the MIR line updates. After `compile`, keep `buffer.get_srclocs_sorted()` per function and register (code range -\u003e line table) with the runtime at define time. The parallel flush in `flush_deferred_defines` has the CompiledCode in hand.\n- Capture: walk the frame-pointer chain (frame pointers are always on in JIT code) from the throw site or the signal context, then map each return address to (function, line).\n- Open questions:\n  - Runtime Rust frames between JIT frames (callbacks): Rust release builds omit frame pointers, so the walk needs the system unwinder or force-frame-pointers for the runtime.\n  - LLVM-tier frames: these need their own line tables, or keep shadow frames there.\n  - Windows x64.\n  - `haxe.CallStack.callStack()` / `exceptionStack()` currently return empty, so only the uncaught-exception report shows the line today.","files":null}]}